WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:01.620
Okay, hear me out.

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.359
If you have enormous amounts of money,
the right thing to do with it

00:00:04.359 --> 00:00:06.420
is to help other people and the world.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:07.420
Obviously.

00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:10.380
But very rich people will likely spend
some money showing off,

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.890
and I can’t help feeling that
if that is going to happen,

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:15.320
then it might be nice if
things like this

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.320
came back into fashion.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.660
In English, the word "water feature"
seems almost quaint.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:21.730
It implies a small fountain
in a back yard

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.410
or a little stream
running down to a pond,

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:27.150
some little babbling-brook diversion
to break up a suburban garden.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.040
But the German translation
for that is "wasserspiele":

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:32.290
literally, "water-games".

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.679
And if you were very rich German nobility
300 years ago,

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:38.100
water-games might include
a massive hillside park,

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.010
a seven-tonne, eleven-metre-high
statue of Hercules,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.620
and a fountain that reaches
fifty metres high.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.499
This is the Water Games of
Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe in Germany,

00:00:48.499 --> 00:00:50.030
it's now a World Heritage Site.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.859
The fountains and water features
here are powered by gravity,

00:00:52.859 --> 00:00:54.199
and controlled on-demand by

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:56.320
hidden mechanisms in
underground pipelines,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.974
some of which are centuries old.

00:00:57.974 --> 00:00:58.550
IN GERMAN:
 
 
 

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.390
By the time you see this video,

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.950
the Wasserspiele will be open to
the public for the summer season.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:39.700
But today, they are just testing.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.810
A few lucky tourists were in the
right place at the right time,

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.690
but the team here didn’t announce
this was happening.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:44.840
Except, they sort of did,
because first,

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.570
there was big release of water at the top
and the cascades that go half way down the hill.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:52.027
And I really wasn’t expecting the noise.

00:01:52.027 --> 00:01:55.284
[LOUD HONK]

00:01:55.284 --> 00:02:00.000
[PITCH AND VOLUME CONTINUE RISING]

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.870
[horn fades] The rushing water brings
a lot of air with it,

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.140
and that’s forced through the trumpets
that the Herald statues hold.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:10.379
The noise is immense, it’s a siren that
for 300 years has told anyone in the park

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:12.608
that the water games are about to begin.

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:31.739
The water goes through
a series of stations,

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:32.779
each a few minutes' walk apart,

00:02:32.779 --> 00:02:35.329
and at each station
the water is released manually,

00:02:35.329 --> 00:02:37.780
at a certain time,
one after the other.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.200
After the cascades, there’s
the artificial waterfalls,

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.660
and then the bridge,
which leads to

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:44.389
the colossal aqueduct,
wind and spray all blowing around

00:02:44.389 --> 00:02:46.030
from the force of the water.

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:47.310
And the crowds of people who’ll be
coming to watch this summer

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.650
will follow along,
walking the path,

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.019
waiting for the water
to be released

00:02:51.019 --> 00:02:52.529
and for each show to begin.

00:02:52.529 --> 00:02:53.859
So I assumed that
this was basically

00:02:53.859 --> 00:02:56.439
one big river with some
engineering in the way.

00:02:56.439 --> 00:02:59.029
But this was built in several parts,
by several different landowners,

00:02:59.029 --> 00:03:01.599
so of course, it's more
complicated than that.

00:03:01.599 --> 00:03:04.960
And it all ends here, at
the fifty-metre fountain.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:51.939
Now, I’m not saying that
building things like this

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:53.810
should be a higher priority
for rich people than

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:56.459
feeding or housing the world,
or any other philanthropy.

00:03:56.459 --> 00:03:57.459
Of course.

00:03:57.459 --> 00:03:59.010
And okay, maybe not fountains,

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.459
maybe some other
big physical art stuff.

00:04:01.459 --> 00:04:04.900
But I can’t help feeling that if someone
is going to spend huge amounts

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.480
on vanity projects to show off
their wealth to the world…

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.959
why not make them spectacular,
and fun, and built to last for centuries?

